1
00:02:26,470 --> 00:02:32,470
Somewhere under the sea and beyond your imagination is an adventure in fantasy.

2
00:04:52,941 --> 00:04:58,941
The Little Mermaid is a animation, family, fantasy film directed by John Musker.

3
00:07:19,411 --> 00:07:25,411
This colorful adventure tells the story of an impetuous mermaid princess named Ariel who falls in love with
the very human Prince Eric and puts everything on the line for the chance to be with him.

4
00:09:45,882 --> 00:09:51,882
Memorable songs and characters -- including the villainous sea witch Ursula.

5
00:12:12,352 --> 00:12:18,352
Ariel / Vanessa (voice), played by Jodi Benson.

6
00:14:38,823 --> 00:14:44,823
Sebastian (voice), played by Samuel E. Wright.

7
00:17:05,294 --> 00:17:11,294
Ursula (voice), played by Pat Carroll.

8
00:19:31,764 --> 00:19:37,764
Prince Eric (voice), played by Christopher Daniel Barnes.

9
00:21:58,235 --> 00:22:04,235
King Triton (voice), played by Kenneth Mars.

10
00:24:24,705 --> 00:24:30,705
Scuttle (voice), played by Buddy Hackett.

11
00:26:51,176 --> 00:26:57,176
The Little Mermaid is a 1989 American animated musical fantasy film written and directed by John Musker and Ron
Clements and produced by Musker and Howard Ashman, who also wrote the film's songs with composer Alan Menken.

12
00:29:17,647 --> 00:29:23,647
Loosely based on the 1837 Danish fairy tale "The Little Mermaid" by Hans Christian Andersen, it was produced by Walt
Disney Feature Animation in association with Silver Screen Partners IV and released by Walt Disney Pictures.

13
00:31:44,117 --> 00:31:50,117
The film features the voices of René Auberjonois, Christopher Daniel Barnes, Jodi Benson, Pat
Carroll, Paddi Edwards, Buddy Hackett, Jason Marin, Kenneth Mars, Ben Wright, and Samuel E.

14
00:34:10,588 --> 00:34:16,588
The story follows a teenage mermaid princess named Ariel who dreams of becoming human and falls in love with a human
prince named Eric, which leads her to forge an agreement with the sea witch Ursula to be with him.

15
00:36:37,058 --> 00:36:43,058
Walt Disney planned to put the story in a proposed package film
containing Andersen's stories, but he scrapped the project.

16
00:39:03,529 --> 00:39:09,529
In 1985, while working on The Great Mouse Detective (1986),
Clements and Musker decided to adapt the fairy tale.

17
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:36,000
They proposed it to Walt Disney Studios chairman Jeffrey Katzenberg, who initially declined due to its
similarities to a proposed sequel to the 1984 film Splash, but ultimately approved it.

18
00:43:56,470 --> 00:44:02,470
Ashman became involved, and brought in Menken.

19
00:46:22,941 --> 00:46:28,941
With supervision from Katzenberg, they made a Broadway-style structure with
musical numbers as the staff was working on Oliver & Company (1988).

20
00:48:49,411 --> 00:48:55,411
Katzenberg warned that the film would earn less since it appealed to female viewers, but he
eventually became convinced that it would be another blockbuster hit for the company.

21
00:51:15,882 --> 00:51:21,882
The Little Mermaid was released in theaters on November 17, 1989, to critical
acclaim, earning praise for the animation, music, and characters.

22
00:53:42,352 --> 00:53:48,352
It was also a commercial success, garnering $84 million at the domestic box office during its initial release, and
$235 million in total lifetime gross worldwide, becoming the sixth-highest-grossing film of 1989.

23
00:56:08,823 --> 00:56:14,823
Along with the major successes of The Great Mouse Detective, the 1988 Disney/Amblin live-action/animated film Who Framed Roger Rabbit and Oliver
& Company, The Little Mermaid is given credit for revitalizing the art of Disney animated feature films after an uneven period.

24
00:58:35,294 --> 00:58:41,294
It also marked the start of the era known as the Disney Renaissance.

25
01:01:01,764 --> 01:01:07,764
The film won two Academy Awards for Best Original Score
and Best Original Song ("Under the Sea").

26
01:03:28,235 --> 01:03:34,235
The film's success led to a media franchise.

27
01:05:54,705 --> 01:06:00,705
A prequel television series aired on CBS from 1992 to 1994.

28
01:08:21,176 --> 01:08:27,176
A direct-to-video sequel was released in 2000,
focusing on Ariel's daughter Melody.

29
01:10:47,647 --> 01:10:53,647
A prequel followed in 2008.

30
01:13:14,117 --> 01:13:20,117
The first film was adapted into a stage musical with a book by Doug Wright and additional songs by Alan Menken and new lyricist
Glenn Slater opened in Denver in July 2007 and began performances on Broadway January 10, 2008 starring Sierra Boggess.

31
01:15:40,588 --> 01:15:46,588
Other derived works and material inspired by the film, include a 2019 live musical presentation on ABC as part of The
Wonderful World of Disney, and a 2023 live-action film adaptation directed by Rob Marshall and starring Halle Bailey.

32
01:18:07,058 --> 01:18:13,058
In 2022, the film was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

33
01:20:33,529 --> 01:20:39,529
The film explores themes of witch, princess, sea, daughter, kingdom.
